 To add Azure AD users to the administrators group in Windows when the system is joined to Azure AD, you can use the `dsadd group` and `dsmod group` commands in PowerShell. Here's a step-by-step guide:

1. Open PowerShell as an administrator.

2. First, find the Azure AD user's Object ID. You can do this by logging into the Azure portal, navigating to the Azure AD users section, selecting the user, and finding the Object ID in the Overview tab.

3. In PowerShell, run the following command to add the user to the built-in Administrators group:

```powershell
$userObjectID = "Your Azure AD user Object ID"
$groupName = "Builtin\Administators"

$user = Get-ADUser -F {objectID -eq $userObjectID} -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
if ($null -eq $user) {
    Write-Host "User not found"
} else {
    $group = Get-ADGroup -Identity $groupName -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
    if ($null -eq $group) {
        Write-Host "Group not found"
    } else {
        Add-ADGroupMember -Identity $group -Members $user
        Write-Host "User added to Administrators group"
    }
}
```

Replace `"Your Azure AD user Object ID"` with the Object ID of the user you want to add to the Administrators group.

This script searches for the user and group, and if both are found, it adds the user to the Administrators group. Make sure to replace the Object ID with the actual Object ID of the Azure AD user.

