 To force a textfield in Adobe to display columns, you can use the Adobe JavaScript Panel (AJP) to create a custom function that splits the text into columns. Here's a simple example of how you can achieve this:

1. Open your Adobe document and press `F9` to open the JavaScript panel.

2. Paste the following code into the JavaScript panel:

```javascript
function textToColumns(text, columns, lineHeight, spaceBetweenColumns) {
    var textArr = text.split("\n");
    var columnsArr = [];
    var column = [];
    var lineHeightPx = lineHeight * emSize;
    var spaceBetweenColumnsPx = spaceBetweenColumns * emSize;

    for (var i = 0; i < textArr.length; i++) {
        var words = textArr[i].split(" ");
        var fit = false;

        for (var j = 0; j < columns && column.length < words.length; j++) {
            var textWidth = measureText(column.join(" ") + " ");
            if (textWidth.width + spaceBetweenColumnsPx < canvas.width) {
                column.push(words[0]);
                fit = true;
                words.shift();
            }
        }

        if (!fit) {
            column.push("\n");
            columnsArr.push(column);
            column = [words[0]];
        }
    }

    if (column.length > 0) {
        columnsArr.push(column);
    }

    var textfield = this.activeDocument.textFrames.add();
    textfield.text = columnsArr.map(function(row) { return row.join(" "); }).join("\n");
    textfield.textRange.appliedParagraphStyle = this.activeDocument.paragraphStyles.getItem("Your Paragraph Style");
    textfield.geometricBounds = {
        width: canvas.width / columns,
        height: textfield.textSize.height * columns + (columns - 1) * spaceBetweenColumnsPx,
        x: 0,
        y: 0
    };
}
```

3. Save the JavaScript file as a .jsx extension (e.g., TextColumns.jsx) in the Adobe Scripts folder (`C:\Program Files\Adobe\Adobe [Your Application]\Adobe [Your Application] CS [Your Version]\Presets\Scripts`).

4. Close and reopen the document where you want to use the script.

5. In the JavaScript panel, click on the `Run` icon (play button) or press `Ctrl+Return` to run the script.

6. In the dialog box, enter the text you want to convert into columns, the number of columns, the line height, and the space between columns.

7. Click `OK` to generate the text with columns in your textfield.

Adjust the code to fit your specific needs and style requirements. This example uses the measureText function to estimate the width of the text and the canvas.width to set the width of the columns. You can customize the paragraph style and the position of the textfield according to your preference.

