 To create a Java method that takes a `List<Car>` and returns the count and color of the most popular car by its color, you can use a combination of sorting and counting. Here's an example implementation:

```java
import java.util.*;

class Car {
    String color;

    public Car(String color) {
        this.color = color;
    }
}

public class PopularCar {
    public static Map<String, Integer> countCarColor(List<Car> cars) {
        Map<String, Integer> colorCountMap = new HashMap<>();

        for (Car car : cars) {
            colorCountMap.put(car.color, colorCountMap.getOrDefault(car.color, 0) + 1);
        }

        return colorCountMap;
    }

    public static Map.Entry<String, Integer> findMostPopularColor(Map<String, Integer> colorCountMap) {
        return colorCountMap.entrySet().stream()
                .max(Map.Entry.comparingByValue())
                .orElse(null);
    }

    public static int getMostPopularCount(Map<String, Integer> colorCountMap) {
        Map.Entry<String, Integer> mostPopular = findMostPopularColor(colorCountMap);
        return mostPopular != null ? mostPopular.getValue() : 0;
    }

    public static String getMostPopularColor(Map<String, Integer> colorCountMap) {
        Map.Entry<String, Integer> mostPopular = findMostPopularColor(colorCountMap);
        return mostPopular != null ? mostPopular.getKey() : "";
    }

    public static int[] getMostPopular(List<Car> cars) {
        Map<String, Integer> colorCountMap = countCarColor(cars);
        Map.Entry<String, Integer> mostPopular = findMostPopularColor(colorCountMap);
        return new int[] {getMostPopularCount(colorCountMap), mostPopular != null ? 1 : 0};
    }
}
```

In this example, we first create a `Car` class to represent a car with a color. Then, we define a `PopularCar` class with static methods to count the occurrences of each color in the list of cars, find the most popular color, and return the count and color of the most popular car.

